 ## Overview
-Iterative Octree Traversal is a method for raycasting against a 3D Sparse Voxel Octree (SVO) that replaces recursive function calls with an explicit stack to comply with hardware constraints (such as HLSL recursion limits) or to optimize CPU cache behavior [S2, S4, unofficial]. It solves the problem of unbounded call stack depth by simulating the traversal state using fixed-size arrays or dynamic stacks, employing a greedy approach that prioritizes the closest node while handling false positives via backtracking [S2, unofficial]. It sets itself apart from [Recursive Octree Traversal](2632-recursive-octree-traversal.md) by explicitly managing the call stack and from [Parametric Octree Traversal](2361-parametric-octree-traversal.md) by often using discrete slab boundaries or DDA steps rather than parametric intervals for child selection [S2, S4, unofficial].
+Iterative Octree Traversal is a method for raycasting against a Sparse Voxel Octree (SVO) that replaces recursive function calls with an explicit stack to manage traversal state [S2, S4, unofficial]. It solves the problem of recursion limits in environments like HLSL or the need for explicit stack management on the CPU, finding the closest voxel intersection by greedily selecting the nearest child node and backtracking on false positives [S2, unofficial]. It sets itself apart from [Recursive Octree Traversal](2632-recursive-octree-traversal.md) by using fixed-size arrays to simulate the call stack and explicitly managing depth and parent pointers for backtracking [S2, unofficial], and from [Parametric Octree Traversal](2361-parametric-octree-traversal.md) by relying on slab-based intersection tests rather than parametric ray equations for child selection.
 
